# PART 5 — PREVENT DUPLICATE GENERATION

Implement a distributed generation locking mechanism using Firestore transactions.

This is essential because several learners may request the same word at the same time.

Example:

Learner A requests "arbeiten".

Learner B requests "arbeiten" at exactly the same time.

The application must generate the audio only once.

Use this workflow:

1. Check if a ready cache entry exists.
2. If not, attempt to acquire a generation lock.
3. Only one request may successfully acquire that lock.
4. The other requests must wait or poll for the result.
5. The successful generator uploads the file to Drive.
6. The Firestore entry is changed to ready.
7. All waiting users retrieve the same shared audio.

The lock must include:

- Generation key.
- Lock owner.
- Created timestamp.
- Expiration timestamp.
- Generation status.

Implement automatic recovery from abandoned locks.

Never leave a cache entry permanently stuck in generating status.

Do not generate duplicate files when an existing upload has already succeeded.

Use transactions and proper error recovery.

